      "artist": "Beyond My Ken",
      "credit": "Own work",
      "source": "14 and 16 St. Nicholas Place from southwest",
      "description": "14 (right, with tower) and 16 (left) St. Nicholas Place, near West 150th Street in the Hamilton Heights area of the Harlem neighborhood of Manhattan, New York City, were built in 1883-84 as residences, and was designed by William Grinnell in the Queen Anne style.  At a time when most of the homes in the area were freestanding, these two detached frame houses were unusual. They are covered in wood shingles, and the tower on #14 is topped by an onion dome.  The two houses are part of New York City's Hamilton Heights/Sugar Hill Northeast Historic District. (Source: Guide to NYC Landmarks (4th ed.))",
